<p>Warner Bros Discovery shareholders voted to approve the company&#x27;s sale to Paramount Skydance, backing a deal valued at about $111 billion including debt and accepting a cash offer of $31 a share. The vote brings one of the biggest recent media deals a step closer to completion and, if regulators also approve it, would put assets including HBO Max, CNN, CBS, and Paramount+ under one corporate roof. Shareholders also rejected an advisory resolution on post-merger executive compensation, signaling discomfort with payouts tied to the transaction even as they backed the merger itself. Critics cited by DW said the tie-up would further concentrate an already tightly held media market, while Warner said it expects the deal to close in the third fiscal quarter pending regulatory review.</p><img src='https://kagiproxy.com/img/adN_W0Pc6cHgzrPbDM4pFpVdf2GB-OboOb2JLVNsh6CyhKpaVHQpnqruD7zHXlu1VebsRlvxDawMHbvtBxd80RLfO3GOsuU7nW6mt1LqydmWtev_WVUu6d2qkRl0OEZTucpgRYvN5OeYrKIBxy7t06bWAexwGcm454g' alt='Image used with reporting on Warner Bros shareholders approving Paramount&#x27;s merger bid' /><br /><h3>Highlights:</h3><ul><li>Executive pay: Warner shareholders voted against an advisory plan that could have allowed CEO David Zaslav to receive as much as $887 million if the transaction closes, after proxy adviser ISS called the potential payout &quot;extremely large&quot;.</li><li>Regulatory hurdle: Management now has a dual task: winning regulatory approval and convincing investors the combined company can deliver the merger&#x27;s promised benefits.</li><li>News assets: If completed, the merger would place CBS and CNN in the same group, a combination critics say would further tighten concentration across the US media landscape.</li></ul><h3>Perspectives:</h3><ul><li>Warner Bros Discovery shareholders: Investors overwhelmingly backed selling the company to Paramount at $31 a share, signaling support for a higher-priced exit after months of competing offers. (<a href='https://michaelwest.com.au/warner-bros-shareholders-approve-paramount-merger/'>Michael West</a>)</li><li>Warner shareholders on pay: The same investors separately rejected the advisory compensation package tied to the merger, drawing a clear line between supporting the sale and endorsing executive rewards. (<a href='https://www.scmp.com/news/world/united-states-canada/article/3351211/warner-bros-investors-back-us110-billion-merger-paramount-skydance'>South China Morning Post</a>)</li><li>Critics cited by DW: Opponents argue the deal would concentrate too much power by combining two major Hollywood studios and bringing CBS and CNN under one roof. (<a href='https://www.dw.com/en/warner-bros-shareholders-greenlight-sale-to-paramount/a-76917707'>DW</a>)</li></ul><h3>Sources:</h3><ul><li><a href='https://michaelwest.com.au/warner-bros-shareholders-approve-paramount-merger/'>Warner Bros shareholders approve Paramount merger</a> - michaelwest.com.au</li><li><a href='https://www.dw.com/en/warner-bros-shareholders-greenlight-sale-to-paramount/a-76917707'>Warner Bros shareholders greenlight sale to Paramount</a> - dw.com</li><li><a href='https://www.bbc.com/news/articles/cgj09ny0dq6o'>Warner Bros shareholders approve Paramount&#x27;s $111bn takeover</a> - bbc.com</li><li><a href='https://www.scmp.com/news/world/united-states-canada/article/3351211/warner-bros-investors-back-us110-billion-merger-paramount-skydance'>Warner Bros investors back US$110 billion merger with Paramount Skydance</a> - scmp.com</li></ul>